{
  // Config for the standalone `typecheck` task (tsc --noEmit).
  //
  // Identical to tsconfig.json except that it drops ".next/types/**/*.ts" from
  // `include` and excludes ".next" outright, which makes typechecking HERMETIC:
  // it reads only checked-in source and never touches build output.
  //
  // Why this exists. tsconfig.json includes ".next/types/**/*.ts" — Next.js
  // itself adds and re-adds that entry on every build, so it cannot simply be
  // removed. Those files are generated by `next build`, so a `typecheck` task
  // and a `build` task running concurrently in the same workspace had tsc
  // reading that directory while next was rewriting it, producing ~66 spurious
  // "error TS6053: File '.next/types/.../page.ts' not found". Ordering build
  // before typecheck would also fix the race, but it would force `next build`
  // into the CI typecheck job — and this app cannot be built without
  // NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL (the SCORM asset-proxy route constructs a Convex
  // client at module scope, so page-data collection throws
  // "Client created with undefined deployment address"). Making typecheck
  // hermetic removes the shared file entirely, so the race is impossible by
  // construction instead of merely ordered.
  //
  // No coverage is lost. CI has always run tsc on a fresh checkout with no
  // .next, so the ".next/types/**" glob matched nothing there and the generated
  // route types were never part of the tsc run. They remain validated by
  // `next build`'s own "Checking validity of types" step, which runs locally
  // and on Vercel.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "include": ["next-env.d.ts", "**/*.ts", "**/*.tsx"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", ".next"]
}
